WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.820
Those big sunglasses can't hide that the actress&nbsp;
who plays Jeffrey Dahmer's mother in Netflix's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.920
"Monster" is probably familiar to lots of viewers.&nbsp;
Here's where you've likely seen her before.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.320
Netflix's new true-crime series "Dahmer&nbsp;
– Monster: The Jeffrey Dahmer Story" once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.640
again seeks to adapt the true story of the&nbsp;
infamous serial killer for the screen. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.380
series was co-created by Ryan Murphy and Ian&nbsp;
Brennan, the minds behind series including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.060
"American Crime Story," "American Horror&nbsp;
Story," and "Scream Queens." Evan Peters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:34.680
who fans may identify as Quicksilver from&nbsp;
the X-Men franchise, stars as Dahmer.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:39.840
Murphy has stressed the importance of centering&nbsp;
Dahmer's victims and their families, as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.640
the institutional racism and ineptitude that&nbsp;
allowed Dahmer to keep killing for as long as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.560
he did. The series goes back into Dahmer's high&nbsp;
school years and features a crop of stars as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.400
disturbed youngster's guardians, who fail to&nbsp;
understand what the young man would become.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:59.460
Richard Jenkins stars as Dahmer's father, Lionel,&nbsp;
and Molly Ringwald plays his stepmother, Shari.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.900
His mother, Joyce, is played by Penelope&nbsp;
Ann Miller, whose first big roles were in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.580
the theater. Her Broadway career began&nbsp;
in a 1985 production of "Biloxi Blues."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:12.900
She reprised the role in the 1988 film&nbsp;
adaptation alongside Matthew Broderick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.040
and she earned a Tony nomination for&nbsp;
the 1989 Broadway revival of "Our Town."

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.540
Big film roles followed suit, and Miller&nbsp;
starred in a number of family-friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:27.600
flicks in the late '80s. In 1987, she co-starred&nbsp;
in "Adventures in Babysitting" as Chris' frenzied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.428
friend Brenda. In 2017, on the movie's&nbsp;
30th anniversary, she reminisced to ET,

00:01:33.428 --> 00:01:36.248
"That was the beginning. That&nbsp;
was the beginning of my career."

00:01:36.248 --> 00:01:41.280
The next year, she appeared in "Big Top Pee-wee"&nbsp;
as Pee-wee Herman's fiancée, Winnie Johnson.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.080
Miller kicked off the 1990s by starring&nbsp;
alongside two gangster-movie legends. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.460
L.A. native put on a thick New York accent&nbsp;
to play Marlon Brando's daughter in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.859
Freshman," a crime comedy that had her once&nbsp;
again sharing scenes with Matthew Broderick.

00:01:54.859 --> 00:01:59.299
"I don't know who. They. It was in&nbsp;
the United States, is my point."

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.720
She also co-starred with Robert De&nbsp;
Niro in the considerably more dramatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:07.860
film "Awakenings." Based on neurologist&nbsp;
Oliver Sacks' memoir of the same name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.960
the film stars Robin Williams as a doctor and&nbsp;
De Niro as an encephalitis patient. Miller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.160
plays another patient's daughter, who starts a&nbsp;
romantic relationship with De Niro's character.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:22.980
Brando and De Niro weren't the only big stars who&nbsp;
Miller rubbed shoulders with in 1990. That year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.340
she also appeared alongside Arnold&nbsp;
Schwarzenegger in "Kindergarten Cop."

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:32.400
In 1993, Miller worked with yet another&nbsp;
"Godfather" star, Al Pacino, in 1993's "Carlito's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:37.500
Way." Directed by Brian de Palma, the film follows&nbsp;
a New York criminal struggling to leave behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.780
his lawbreaking ways. For her role as Carlito's&nbsp;
girlfriend, Gail, Miller earned a Golden Globe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.700
nomination for Best Actress. Miller was initially&nbsp;
dubious about the character, who is a stripper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.960
But ultimately, it was too good an opportunity&nbsp;
to pass up. She told The Baltimore Sun,

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:56.700
"I figured I could meet with Pacino at least,&nbsp;
and he could see my work. I could establish a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.660
relationship with him and Brian DePalma.&nbsp;
But I realized that this was a great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.660
meaty role and that the stripping&nbsp;
was such a very small part of it."

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:08.880
Miller then went on to star in the 1997 creature&nbsp;
feature "The Relic." After that, she returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.320
to Broadway and made TV appearances in series&nbsp;
including "The Closer" and "Desperate Housewives."

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.700
In 2011, she had a supporting role in&nbsp;
"The Artist." The nostalgic throwback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.760
to silent-film-era Hollywood&nbsp;
cleaned up during awards season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.140
winning three Golden Globe Awards and five&nbsp;
Academy Awards, including Best Picture.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:29.100
In the film, Miller plays Doris,&nbsp;
the embattled wife of silent film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:33.540
star George Valentin. Of the challenges&nbsp;
of silent acting, Miller told MakingOf,

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.320
"I think the daunting task is to&nbsp;
be believable and authentic to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:41.280
period. [...] You don't want to overdo it and&nbsp;
seem like you're over-acting or being gimmicky."

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.800
Miller was perhaps the only "Artist" cast member&nbsp;
to have any sort of silent acting experience. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.503
played silent film actress Edna Purviance in&nbsp;
1992's "Chaplin" alongside Robert Downey Jr.

